<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis I, Prince of Anhalt-Köthen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1579">1579</a>–<a href="/years/1650">1650</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Ludwig I of Anhalt-Köthen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis I, Prince of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a> from 1603 until his death, was the first ruler of the separate principality of Anhalt-Köthen. In 1617 he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fruitbearing-society">Fruchtbringende Gesellschaft</a>, the first society devoted to refining and standardising the German language, and he made Köthen a centre of learning and educational reform. His principality suffered heavily in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-i-prince-of-anhalt-kothen.jpg" alt="Louis I, Prince of Anhalt-Köthen" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ludwig_I._von_Anhalt-K%C3%B6then.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Louis was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> on 17 June 1579, a prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ascania">House of Ascania</a> and the seventh son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-ernest-prince-of-anhalt">Joachim Ernest, Prince of Anhalt</a>, by his second wife Eleonore, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christoph-duke-of-wurttemberg">Christoph, Duke of Württemberg</a>. When his father died in 1586 he inherited Anhalt jointly with his brothers and half-brothers, and he grew up at Dessau at the court of his elder brother and guardian, John George I.</p><p>From 1596 he made a Grand Tour through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where he stayed until 1602. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, with the help of his Italian tutor Bastiano de' Rossi, he became the first German member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accademia-della-crusca">Accademia della Crusca</a>, under the name L'Acceso.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1603 the surviving co-rulers agreed a formal division of Anhalt, and Louis received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a>, where he made his residence. Cautious in military and political matters, he promoted agriculture vigorously, built a new Schloss in the Italian style and had his court adopt Italian manners. In 1619 he funded an educational initiative in Köthen under Wolfgang Ratke, but quarrels with the local clergy led to Ratke's imprisonment for eight months, and the project was left incomplete.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fruitbearing-society">Fruitbearing Society</a> was proposed by the seneschal Kaspar von Teutleben at the funeral of Louis's sister Dorothea Maria, Duchess of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, on 24 August 1617, on the model of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accademia-della-crusca">Accademia della Crusca</a>. Louis was made its first head and led it until his death, known within it as Der Nährende, the nourisher. During the Thirty Years' War King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> gave him the governorship of the dioceses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halberstadt">Halberstadt</a>, which angered Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axel-oxenstierna">Axel Oxenstierna</a>, who wanted the post and was himself a member of the society.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Louis married Countess Amöena Amalie of Bentheim-Steinfurt in 1606; their son Louis died in 1624 and their daughter Louise Amöena in 1625, the year his wife also died. In 1626 he married Countess Sophie of Lippe, daughter of Simon VI, Count of Lippe, who bore him William Louis in 1638. Louis died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a> on 7 January 1650; his son became William Louis, Prince of Anhalt-Köthen, and died in 1665.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis I of Anhalt-Köthen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.83,12.25&amp;year=1625" aria-label="Dessau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Louis I, Prince of Anhalt-Köthen"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5642 5636h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5642 5636h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.83,12.25&amp;year=1625">What was here in 1625? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
